    <title phx-r data-default="Copycat Cafe">9 common ways to say &quot;Good morning&quot; in French</title>
    <meta name="description" content="">
    
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://copycatcafe.com/blog/good-morning-french">
    <meta property="og:title" content="9 common ways to say &quot;Good morning&quot; in French">
    <meta property="og:description" content="">
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://copycatcafe.com/blog/good-morning-french">
    <meta property="og:type" content="article">
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Copycat Cafe">
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://copycatcafe.com/og-image.jpg">
    <meta property="article:published_time" content="2022-06-20T11:12:50.000000Z">
    <meta property="article:modified_time" content="2026-05-29T10:42:42.075846Z">
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="9 common ways to say &quot;Good morning&quot; in French">
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="">
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://copycatcafe.com/og-image.jpg">
    <script nonce="gXEqRoUUgaG89QFnnizmsw" type="application/ld+json">
{"@context":"https://schema.org","@id":"https://copycatcafe.com/blog/good-morning-french#article","@type":"Article","articleBody":"There’s no specific way to say “Good morning” in French - at least not in French spoken in France. This may seem especially surprising since the French are extremely polite and have a number of different ways of saying “Hello” and ways of saying “Goodbye” for particular situations and times of day.   It’s not that the French don’t greet each other in the morning; they just use greetings you know already, as well as some other phrases. So if you want to start your day by greeting your favorite French person the proper way, don’t despair! Let’s look at nine ways to say “Good morning” in French, including one exception to that whole “no way to say ‘Good morning’ thing”. If you want to greet people in French and actually sound like a native, Copycat Cafe scores your pronunciation on every phrase with AI feedback. Try it free for 7 days. 9 ways to say “Good morning” in French The standard “Good morning”: Bonjour If you break it down, Bonjour, the French go-to greeting, means “good” (bon) and “day” (jour). That’s why it works perfectly for “Good morning”. When you think about it, it’s maybe even better than “Good morning”, since you’re wishing someone an entire good day. Bonjour first thing in the morning might seem weirdly formal to you at first. I know that was the case for me. But I can tell you firsthand that it’s what French people really do say to each other. For instance, when we’re not just nodding to each other in a tired fog, my husband will greet me with Bonjour. So this is the best French “Good morning” for formal or informal occasions and the first greeting we teach in our conversational French course. The “ ‘morning” “Good morning”: Salut   If you want a less formal “Good morning”, Salut, which usually means “Hi” in French, is the way to go. You could say it’s the rough equivalent of “ ‘morning”. With this in mind, I would only use this with friends and other people I’m close to. Otherwise, sticking to Bonjour is probably the better idea. The flirty “Good morning”: Salut toi You may come upon a scene in a French TV show, movie, or book, where one person greets another the morning after they’ve had a particularly romance-filled night, by saying Salut toi. This roughly translates to “Hey, you.” Depending on how it’s said, this phrase can run the gamut of registers, from a gruff greeting that still expresses some sort of fondness, to flirty and cute, to downright sexy. But as with anything that could be flirty or sexy, it could come off silly, so try to “read the room”. It might help to watch and read things in French to notice when and how it’s used and said. And anyway, you’re certainly not required to say this - it’s just a good idea to at least recognize it, since you’re likely to come across it at some point. Note that in general, Salut toi can also be a pickup line, sort of like “Hey there,” or “Well, hello there!” or even “Hey, baby.” On a totally different level, Salut toi is sometimes used to address little children (and sometimes pets), sort of like saying, “Good morning, you,” or “Hello there, you.” As for using it for a pickup line, be aware that this will probably come off as disrespectful to most people, and if that doesn’t deter you, since 2018, under French law, you could be fined for street harassment. So, save Salut toi for someone you know. The caring “Good morning”: Tu as bien dormi? / Vous avez bien dormi? I often hear Tu as bien dormi? (or, if a group is being addressed or it’s in a formal setting like a hotel, Vous avez bien dormi?) as a morning greeting in France. This phrase, which means “Did you sleep well?”,  can be used among family members or friends. It can be also be asked by a host to their guests. The kindness of the comment is a nice way to start the day, both for the listener, and for the person being asked! Note that this greeting is usually used at the place where you slept or maybe just after leaving it. So it’s not a good one to say, for instance, if you have a morning meeting with a friend or coworker. The “Have a good morning”: Bonne journ é e What if you don’t want to say “Good morning” as a greeting, so much as wish someone a good morning? There is a phrase for that: Bonne journée. Like Bonjour, Bonne journée covers the whole day. But maybe you could think of that as being generous? In addition to this option, I’ve come across specific situations where people will use the phrase bonne matinée. Unlike Bonne journée, this phrase is rarely ever used on its own. You’ll most commonly hear it in statements like: Passe une bonne matinée./Passez une bonne matinée. (Have a nice morning.) or Je vous souhaite une bonne matinée. (I wish you a good morning/I hope you have a good morning.). Bonne matinée is far less common than Bonne journée. So if in doubt, the latter is the way to go. The Quebec “Good morning”: Bon matin   In French spoken in mainland France, there is no exact translation or specific greeting for “Good morning”, although we’ve just seen several ways to ex","author":{"@id":"https://copycatcafe.com/blog/author/alysa-salzberg","@type":"Person","description":"Alysa Salzberg is an American writer and cookie enthusiast who has lived in Paris, France for over a decade. She's the author of Hearts at Dawn, a Historical Novel Society Editors' Choice book that retells Beauty and the Beast during the 1870 Siege of Paris. She brings a unique perspective to French language and culture from her years living in France.","name":"Alysa Salzberg","sameAs":["https://www.alysasalzberg.com/","https://medium.com/@AlysaSalzberg"],"url":"https://copycatcafe.com/blog/author/alysa-salzberg"},"breadcrumb":{"@id":"https://copycatcafe.com/blog/good-morning-french#breadcrumb"},"dateModified":"2026-05-29T10:42:42.075846Z","datePublished":"2022-06-20T11:12:50.000000Z","description":"","headline":"9 common ways to say \"Good morning\" in French","image":"https://copycatcafe.com/og-image.jpg","isPartOf":{"@id":"https://copycatcafe.com/#website"},"mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://copycatcafe.com/blog/good-morning-french#webpage","@type":"WebPage"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://copycatcafe.com/#organization","@type":"Organization","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://copycatcafe.com/logo.png"},"name":"Copycat Cafe"},"url":"https://copycatcafe.com/blog/good-morning-french","wordCount":1314}
    </script><script nonce="gXEqRoUUgaG89QFnnizmsw" type="application/ld+json">
{"@context":"https://schema.org","@id":"https://copycatcafe.com/blog/good-morning-french#breadcrumb","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","item":"https://copycatcafe.com/","name":"Home","position":1},{"@type":"ListItem","item":"https://copycatcafe.com/blog","name":"Blog","position":2},{"@type":"ListItem","item":"https://copycatcafe.com/blog/category/french-vocabulary","name":"French Vocabulary & Phrases","position":3},{"@type":"ListItem","item":"https://copycatcafe.com/blog/good-morning-french","name":"9 common ways to say \"Good morning\" in French","position":4}]}
    </script><script nonce="gXEqRoUUgaG89QFnnizmsw" type="application/ld+json">
{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The standard way is 'bonjour,' which is used from morning through afternoon. There is no separate French word that exclusively means 'good morning' the way English distinguishes it from 'good afternoon.' In very casual settings, you might hear 'salut' (hi) in the morning, but bonjour is always appropriate."},"name":"How do you say good morning in French?"},{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Bonjour is the correct and universally used French greeting for morning and daytime. Bon matin exists but is primarily used in Québécois French (Canadian French), not in France. Saying bon matin in France would sound unusual or foreign. Stick with bonjour for standard French."},"name":"What is the difference between bonjour and bon matin?"},{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The switch from bonjour to bonsoir (good evening) typically happens around 6 PM, though it can vary. In winter when it gets dark earlier, some people switch to bonsoir as early as 5 PM. In summer, bonjour might be used until 7 PM. There is no strict rule — it roughly follows when evening feels like it has begun."},"name":"When do you stop saying bonjour and start saying bonsoir?"}]}
    </script>
    <script nonce="gXEqRoUUgaG89QFnnizmsw">
      document.documentElement.dataset.javascript = "enabled"
    </script>
